   "fen": "rn1qk2r/1b2bppp/p2ppn2/1pp5/4PP2/P1NP1NP1/1PP3BP/R1BQ1RK1 b kq - 4 9",
   "line": [
    "O-O"
   ],
   "spoken": "This is the moment for the queen to c7, and it's played for layered reasons: it connects the rooks, and paired with the knight on d7 it double-guards e5 — smothering the central break that would otherwise be so dangerous. It's simply a better home than the back rank.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 18,
   "t": 253.5,
   "fen": "r2qk2r/1b1nbppp/p2ppn2/1pp5/4PP2/P1NP1NP1/1PP3BP/R1BQ1RK1 w kq - 5 10",
   "line": [
    "Nbd7"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 19,
   "t": 261,
   "fen": "r2qk2r/1b1nbppp/p2ppn2/1pp5/4PP2/P1NP1NPP/1PP3B1/R1BQ1RK1 b kq - 0 10",
   "line": [
    "h3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 20,
   "t": 268,
   "fen": "r3k2r/1bqnbppp/p2ppn2/1pp5/4PP2/P1NP1NPP/1PP3B1/R1BQ1RK1 w kq - 1 11",
   "line": [
    "Qc7"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 21,
   "t": 284,
   "fen": "r3k2r/1bqnbppp/p2ppn2/1pp5/4PP2/P2P1NPP/1PP1N1B1/R1BQ1RK1 b kq - 2 11",
   "line": [
    "Ne2"
   ],
   "spoken": "You'll feel the kinship with a hedgehog or hippo here — the d6-and-e6 pawns give that flavour, though it's really its own animal. And a castling caution: going long is risky, since those queenside pawns are hooks the opponent can lever open; short is safer, though long is never off the table.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 22,
   "t": 324.5,
   "fen": "r3k2r/1bqnbpp1/p2ppn2/1pp4p/4PP2/P2P1NPP/1PP1N1B1/R1BQ1RK1 w kq - 0 12",
   "line": [
    "h5"
   ],
   "spoken": "A deliberately provocative move — h5 to stop the kingside pawn expansion, keeping the king parked in the centre for now. It's risky, but the aim is to be as annoying as possible and see how the opponent reacts.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 23,
   "t": 354,
   "fen": "r3k2r/1bqnbpp1/p2ppn2/1pp4p/P3PP2/3P1NPP/1PP1N1B1/R1BQ1RK1 b kq - 0 12",
   "line": [
    "a4"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 24,
   "t": 365,
   "fen": "r3k2r/1bqnbpp1/p2ppn2/2p4p/Pp2PP2/3P1NPP/1PP1N1B1/R1BQ1RK1 w kq - 0 13",
   "line": [
    "b4"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 25,
   "t": 369.5,
   "fen": "r3k2r/1bqnbpp1/p2ppn2/2p4p/Pp2PP2/2PP1NPP/1P2N1B1/R1BQ1RK1 b kq - 0 13",
   "line": [
    "c3"
   ],
   "spoken": "When the opponent props the centre with c3, the thing to notice is that it has quietly weakened d3. And here's the key insight — you don't only attack a weak pawn with pieces, you undermine it with your own pawns. That points toward a c-pawn thrust to c4.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 26,
   "t": 419.5,
   "fen": "r3k2r/1bqnbpp1/p2ppn2/2p4p/P3PP2/2pP1NPP/1P2N1B1/R1BQ1RK1 w kq - 0 14",
   "line": [
    "bxc3"
   ],
   "spoken": "But capture on c3 first — playing the thrust immediately would leave the advanced b-pawn hanging. A small preparatory recapture, and then the undermining pawn goes in with a clear conscience.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 27,
   "t": 427.5,
   "fen": "r3k2r/1bqnbpp1/p2ppn2/2p4p/P3PP2/2PP1NPP/4N1B1/R1BQ1RK1 b kq - 0 14",
   "line": [
    "bxc3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 28,
   "t": 428.5,
   "fen": "r3k2r/1bqnbpp1/p2ppn2/7p/P1p1PP2/2PP1NPP/4N1B1/R1BQ1RK1 w kq - 0 15",
   "line": [
    "c4"
   ],
   "spoken": "Now the pawn arrives and the centre creaks. See how the minor pieces suddenly make sense — the queen guards the pawn, the bishop and knight both rake e4, and where they were biting on granite before, d3 is now a glaring weakness. That's the danger of shrinking your own pawn chain: the fewer links, the easier it is to undermine.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 29,
   "t": 460,
   "fen": "r3k2r/1bqnbpp1/p2ppn2/6Np/P1p1PP2/2PP2PP/4N1B1/R1BQ1RK1 b kq - 1 15",
   "line": [
    "Ng5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 30,
   "t": 494,
   "fen": "r3k2r/1bq1bpp1/p2ppn2/2n3Np/P1p1PP2/2PP2PP/4N1B1/R1BQ1RK1 w kq - 2 16",
   "line": [
    "Nc5"
   ],
   "spoken": "Don't snatch the weak pawn on reflex — the move is a knight to c5, bringing a piece into the attack and piling on d3, now genuinely threatening the capture. If the pawn advances to dodge, the central capture on e4 forces matters.",
   "kind": "tactic"
  },
  {
   "ply": 29,
   "t": 516,
   "fen": "r3k2r/1bqnbpp1/p2ppn2/6Np/P1p1PP2/2PP2PP/4N1B1/R1BQ1RK1 b kq - 1 15",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 30,
   "t": 544.5,
   "fen": "r3k2r/1bq1bpp1/p2ppn2/2n3Np/P1p1PP2/2PP2PP/4N1B1/R1BQ1RK1 w kq - 2 16",
   "line": [
    "Nc5"
   ],
   "spoken": "The reason not to grab first is patience: taking immediately lets the queen into the game, and the tempting knight jump attacking it just shoos it to safety, cutting your targets from two down to one. Leave the tension, keep both weaknesses, and let the opponent stew in the ultimatum.",
   "kind": "plan",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 31,
   "t": 614.5,
   "fen": "r3k2r/1bq1bpp1/p2ppn2/2n1P1Np/P1p2P2/2PP2PP/4N1B1/R1BQ1RK1 b kq - 0 16",
   "line": [
    "e5"
   ],
   "spoken": "And a permission worth hearing: you're allowed not to castle at all. When the centre is closed and both wings look dangerous, keeping the king in the middle can be the lesser evil — the only real cost is that the rooks stay disconnected, which is rarely mission-critical.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 32,
   "t": 653.5,
   "fen": "r3k2r/2q1bpp1/p2ppn2/2n1P1Np/P1p2P2/2PP2PP/4N1b1/R1BQ1RK1 w kq - 0 17",
   "line": [
    "Bxg2"
   ],
   "spoken": "The centre bursts open, and now it's pure order of operations. Trade on g2 first — that eliminates the bishop headache, and if the opponent grabs the knight the rook is simply captured with an extra exchange in hand. Clear the simplest question before weighing the harder one.",
   "kind": "tactic"
  },
  {
   "ply": 33,
   "t": 659,
   "fen": "r3k2r/2q1bpp1/p2ppn2/2n1P1Np/P1p2P2/2PP2PP/4N1K1/R1BQ1R2 b kq - 0 17",
   "line": [
    "Kxg2"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 34,
   "t": 713,
   "fen": "r3k2r/2q1bpp1/p2ppn2/2n1P1Np/P4P2/2Pp2PP/4N1K1/R1BQ1R2 w kq - 0 18",
   "line": [
    "cxd3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
