   ],
   "spoken": "Why trade the light-squared bishops? Purely to strip the enemy king of its defender — king safety outranks any square complex here. Since the g-file and the e4 outpost are already secured, removing the bishop from beside the king guarantees there's no extra guard for the weak point in front of it.",
   "kind": "plan",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 34,
   "t": 2031,
   "fen": "r3k2r/2q1bpp1/p2ppn2/2n1P1Np/P4P2/2Pp2PP/4N1K1/R1BQ1R2 w kq - 0 18",
   "line": [
    "cxd3"
   ],
   "spoken": "And why the e4 outpost is safe even if the knight is challenged by a retreat: with a rook already on the g-file the pawn beside the king hangs, so there's no time for the challenge. Even if there were, the outpost can be re-occupied by a protected passed pawn, or reinforced by the queen, or simply cashed for the free pawn on c3. There's always an answer.",
   "kind": "plan",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 24,
   "t": 2161,
   "fen": "r3k2r/1bqnbpp1/p2ppn2/2p4p/Pp2PP2/3P1NPP/1PP1N1B1/R1BQ1RK1 w kq - 0 13",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": "A closing technical note on the endgame trade: knights are the worst piece at halting a passed rook-pawn, so hesitation is natural — but here the king sits so close to the pawn that it hardly matters what stops it. Judge the piece by the concrete race, not the general rule.",
   "reanchor": true
  }
